WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.400
Pollen from one pea plant is brushed onto

00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:04.000
the flower of another.

00:00:04.080 --> 00:00:06.160
A few weeks later, the pod is full

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:08.800
of seeds, and every seed has the same

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:09.120
two

00:00:09.640 --> 00:00:10.240
parents.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:12.400
Yet the seeds aren't all alike.

00:00:12.480 --> 00:00:15.440
Some are yellow, some green, some round, some

00:00:15.480 --> 00:00:16.560
wrinkled.

00:00:16.640 --> 00:00:18.800
So why are offspring of the same parents

00:00:18.960 --> 00:00:20.240
different from each other?

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:22.000
And could one of them carry an allele

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:23.600
that neither parent had?

00:00:24.800 --> 00:00:27.680
There are four sources of genetic variation.

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:30.320
Three of them you've met: crossing over and

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:34.720
independent assortment in meiosis, and random fertilisation.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:38.720
Those three reshuffle alleles that already exist, like

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.600
dealing new hands from the same deck of

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:41.200
cards.

00:00:41.840 --> 00:00:44.240
The fourth, mutation, is different.

00:00:44.720 --> 00:00:47.760
It changes the DNA itself, so it's the

00:00:47.840 --> 00:00:50.480
only one that can make a brand-new allele,

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:51.840
a new card in the deck.

00:00:53.067 --> 00:00:55.826
Let's trace where a seed's alleles come from.

00:00:56.107 --> 00:00:58.266
Take one parent plant and two of its

00:00:58.347 --> 00:01:00.826
genes, on two different chromosomes.

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:03.066
It got one set of chromosomes from each

00:01:03.147 --> 00:01:05.946
of its own parents: capital Y and capital

00:01:06.027 --> 00:01:08.826
R from one, small y and small r

00:01:08.907 --> 00:01:09.466
from the other.

00:01:09.947 --> 00:01:12.426
When it makes gametes, each pair lines up

00:01:12.507 --> 00:01:13.946
either way, on its own.

00:01:14.347 --> 00:01:16.426
So a gamete can get Y with R,

00:01:16.507 --> 00:01:18.626
or small y with small r, like the

00:01:18.667 --> 00:01:19.386
grandparents.

00:01:19.787 --> 00:01:22.586
Or it can get two new mixes: Y

00:01:22.667 --> 00:01:25.066
with small r, and small y with R.

00:01:25.547 --> 00:01:28.026
If both ways are equally likely, and nothing

00:01:28.107 --> 00:01:30.666
mutates, each kind is a quarter of the

00:01:30.787 --> 00:01:31.386
gametes.

00:01:32.600 --> 00:01:34.760
But what about two genes on the same

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:35.640
chromosome?

00:01:36.120 --> 00:01:39.480
Assortment moves whole chromosomes, so on its own

00:01:39.720 --> 00:01:42.440
it would keep their alleles together, always capital

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:44.920
A with capital B, and small a with

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:45.880
small b.

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:48.360
Crossing over is what splits them up.

00:01:48.920 --> 00:01:50.920
If the swap you saw in meiosis happens

00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:53.720
between the two genes, it makes chromatids with

00:01:53.800 --> 00:01:56.520
capital A and small b, and small a

00:01:56.680 --> 00:01:57.800
with capital B.

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.120
A swap in that stretch happens in only

00:02:00.280 --> 00:02:03.400
some cells, so these new combinations are usually

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:04.280
less common.

00:02:05.500 --> 00:02:07.140
Now fertilisation.

00:02:07.420 --> 00:02:09.980
In a pea flower, each ovule is reached

00:02:10.060 --> 00:02:12.300
by its own pollen tube, from its own

00:02:12.380 --> 00:02:15.100
pollen grain, and which grain gets there is

00:02:15.180 --> 00:02:16.060
chance.

00:02:16.380 --> 00:02:18.300
So each seed in the pod comes from

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.380
a different pair of gametes.

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:22.540
With four kinds of pollen and four kinds

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:25.900
of egg, that's sixteen possible pairings for just

00:02:26.060 --> 00:02:27.580
these two genes.

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:30.060
For people, each parent can make more than

00:02:30.140 --> 00:02:33.740
8 million chromosome combinations, so one couple has

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:37.740
about 70 trillion possible pairings, before crossing over

00:02:37.900 --> 00:02:38.940
adds even more.

00:02:40.167 --> 00:02:42.966
That's why brothers and sisters, or seeds in

00:02:43.047 --> 00:02:46.006
one pod, share alleles but differ.

00:02:46.327 --> 00:02:48.486
Every allele they carry came from the same

00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:51.686
two parents, so they're alike in many ways.

00:02:51.767 --> 00:02:54.246
But each got a different combination, from different

00:02:54.327 --> 00:02:57.686
gametes, so two siblings are almost never genetically

00:02:57.887 --> 00:02:58.806
identical.

00:02:58.887 --> 00:03:01.366
No new mutation is needed to explain it.

00:03:02.567 --> 00:03:05.046
So where does a new allele come from?

00:03:05.527 --> 00:03:08.486
From a mutation, a change in the DNA's

00:03:08.607 --> 00:03:11.046
base sequence, such as a copying error that

00:03:11.127 --> 00:03:12.246
isn't repaired.

00:03:12.567 --> 00:03:15.206
In 1910, in a lab full of red-eyed

00:03:15.287 --> 00:03:18.686
fruit flies, one male hatched with white eyes.

00:03:18.967 --> 00:03:21.366
A new allele had appeared, and he passed

00:03:21.447 --> 00:03:22.766
it on to his offspring.

00:03:23.047 --> 00:03:25.206
That only works if the mutation is in

00:03:25.247 --> 00:03:27.366
a cell that makes eggs or sperm.

00:03:27.767 --> 00:03:30.166
A mutation in a fly's gut cell stays

00:03:30.207 --> 00:03:31.046
in that fly.

00:03:32.267 --> 00:03:34.346
Not every difference is genetic, though.

00:03:34.827 --> 00:03:37.466
Remember the hydrangea cuttings, clones with the same

00:03:37.547 --> 00:03:39.866
genotype, that flowered blue or pink?

00:03:40.507 --> 00:03:43.746
Differences between clones come from the environment.

00:03:44.187 --> 00:03:46.986
Differences between seeds from a cross are mostly

00:03:47.067 --> 00:03:48.906
genetic, from the four sources.

00:03:49.387 --> 00:03:51.146
To tell them apart, grow them in the

00:03:51.307 --> 00:03:54.146
same conditions, or compare clones.

00:03:54.427 --> 00:03:57.706
And only genetic differences are inherited, because the

00:03:57.787 --> 00:04:00.746
environment doesn't change the alleles in the gametes.

00:04:01.967 --> 00:04:03.566
Why does any of this matter?

00:04:04.207 --> 00:04:08.126
Variation means that when conditions change, some individuals

00:04:08.207 --> 00:04:10.926
are more likely to survive and reproduce, so

00:04:10.967 --> 00:04:14.126
the population is more likely to keep going.

00:04:14.207 --> 00:04:17.486
In any one generation, crossing over, assortment and

00:04:17.567 --> 00:04:21.486
fertilisation make huge numbers of new combinations.

00:04:21.807 --> 00:04:24.286
Mutation is rare for any one gene, but

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:27.486
over long periods it supplies every new allele

00:04:27.567 --> 00:04:29.486
that the reshuffling then mixes.

00:04:30.700 --> 00:04:35.300
To remember the four: Cross, Independently, Randomly,

00:04:35.500 --> 00:04:39.900
for crossing over, independent assortment and random fertilisation,

00:04:39.940 --> 00:04:41.660
the three that reshuffle.

00:04:41.740 --> 00:04:43.900
And only Mutation makes new.

00:04:45.100 --> 00:04:46.700
Two harder questions now.

00:04:47.180 --> 00:04:49.980
First, a cell in a fruit fly's ovary

00:04:50.060 --> 00:04:51.460
is making eggs.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:54.620
On one chromosome pair, it has alleles D

00:04:54.700 --> 00:04:57.740
and E on one chromosome, and small d

00:04:57.900 --> 00:04:59.740
and small e on its partner.

00:05:00.380 --> 00:05:02.940
On another pair, it has F and small

00:05:03.100 --> 00:05:03.780
f.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:06.540
List the combinations its eggs can carry if

00:05:06.620 --> 00:05:08.060
there's no crossing over.

00:05:08.700 --> 00:05:11.900
Then list the extra combinations if crossing over

00:05:11.940 --> 00:05:14.460
happens between the D and E genes, and

00:05:14.540 --> 00:05:16.380
explain why those are less common.

00:05:17.020 --> 00:05:18.300
Pause here to answer.

00:05:28.734 --> 00:05:31.613
With no crossing over, capital D stays with

00:05:31.694 --> 00:05:34.973
capital E, and small d with small e,

00:05:35.054 --> 00:05:37.693
and each can go with either F allele:

00:05:37.774 --> 00:05:39.533
that's four combinations.

00:05:39.854 --> 00:05:42.573
Crossing over between the genes puts capital D

00:05:42.654 --> 00:05:45.213
with small e, and small d with capital

00:05:45.294 --> 00:05:47.453
E, which gives four more.

00:05:47.494 --> 00:05:49.693
They're less common because that swap happens in

00:05:49.774 --> 00:05:51.533
only some cells.

00:05:51.614 --> 00:05:54.253
If you listed all eight as equally common,

00:05:54.334 --> 00:05:56.253
you treated D and E as if they

00:05:56.294 --> 00:05:58.013
were on different chromosomes.

00:05:59.234 --> 00:05:59.873
Second.

00:06:00.274 --> 00:06:02.353
A grower has one strawberry plant.

00:06:02.914 --> 00:06:05.233
Group A is twenty plants grown from its

00:06:05.314 --> 00:06:07.873
runners: ten in a sunny bed and ten

00:06:07.954 --> 00:06:09.313
in a shady bed.

00:06:09.394 --> 00:06:11.873
Their fruit size differs between the beds.

00:06:12.194 --> 00:06:14.673
Group B is twenty plants grown from its

00:06:14.754 --> 00:06:17.553
seeds, after its flowers were pollinated by another

00:06:17.634 --> 00:06:20.433
strawberry plant, all in the sunny bed.

00:06:20.554 --> 00:06:23.873
Their fruit size, shape and colour vary.

00:06:23.994 --> 00:06:26.193
Explain the source of the variation in each

00:06:26.274 --> 00:06:27.153
group.

00:06:27.434 --> 00:06:29.873
Then say which group's differences could be passed

00:06:29.994 --> 00:06:32.433
on through seeds, and justify.

00:06:32.594 --> 00:06:33.873
Pause here to answer.

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.660
Group A are clones, with the same genotype,

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:50.260
so their differences come from the environment.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:53.020
Group B grew in the same bed, so

00:06:53.100 --> 00:06:56.460
their differences are mostly genetic: each seed came

00:06:56.580 --> 00:07:00.300
from different gametes, reshuffled by crossing over, assortment

00:07:00.380 --> 00:07:02.380
and random fertilisation.

00:07:02.540 --> 00:07:05.660
Only Group B's differences can be passed on.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:07.660
If you said the shady plants will pass

00:07:07.740 --> 00:07:11.180
on small fruit, no: the shade didn't change

00:07:11.260 --> 00:07:12.060
their alleles.

00:07:13.267 --> 00:07:15.826
So when a question asks where variation comes

00:07:15.907 --> 00:07:17.986
from, sort it first.

00:07:18.307 --> 00:07:21.186
New combinations of existing alleles come from crossing

00:07:21.267 --> 00:07:24.066
over, assortment and fertilisation.

00:07:24.387 --> 00:07:26.626
A new allele needs a mutation, and to

00:07:26.707 --> 00:07:28.626
be passed on, it must be in the

00:07:28.707 --> 00:07:29.986
egg or sperm line.

00:07:30.467 --> 00:07:32.626
And if the genotypes are the same, look

00:07:32.667 --> 00:07:33.586
to the environment.

00:07:34.307 --> 00:07:37.506
Next, you'll model these processes yourself, and then

00:07:37.667 --> 00:07:39.826
predict offspring with Punnett squares.

00:07:41.034 --> 00:07:42.473
Here are the key notes.

00:07:42.714 --> 00:07:44.633
Pause the video if you need more time

00:07:44.714 --> 00:07:45.513
to copy them.
